# [Queries in .sql files]($heading.id('sql-files'))

Every `.sql` file under a configured SQL root holds one query. Header comments
describe it:

```sql
-- sqlz.name: add_user
-- sqlz.backends: sqlite, postgres
-- sqlz.cardinality: exec

INSERT INTO users(name) VALUES (:name);
```

The generated module exposes it under the root alias and its directory, for
example `@import("queries").app.basic_sql_file.add_user`.

# [Queries in Zig]($heading.id('zig-declarations'))

Declare a query next to the code that runs it. The checker finds every
`sqlz.Query` under the configured Zig roots and compares the `.params` and
`.row` structs with the SQL: field names, order, nullability and type.

```zig
pub const list_users = sqlz.Query(.{
    .sql = "SELECT id, name FROM users ORDER BY id",
    .backends = .{ .sqlite = true, .postgres = true },
    .cardinality = .many,
    .row = struct { id: i64, name: []const u8 },
});
```

# [Cardinality]($heading.id('cardinality'))

| Cardinality | Method | Returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `exec` | `execute` | rows affected, no rows |
| `one` | `fetchOne` | exactly one row, or an error |
| `optional` | `fetchOptional` | zero or one row |
| `many` | `fetch` | a stream of rows |

# [Parameters]($heading.id('parameters'))

Parameters are named, `:name`, on both backends. sqlz rewrites them for the
driver and infers each parameter's type from where it's used.

# [Results and errors]($heading.id('results'))

Every database call returns a `sqlz.Result`: `.ok` with the value, or `.err`
with the backend's error class, code and message. `sqlz.unwrap` turns it into
a Zig error when the details don't matter:

```zig
switch (add_user.execute(&conn, .{ .name = "Ada" })) {
    .ok => {},
    .err => |err| {
        defer err.deinit();
        std.log.err("insert failed: {s}", .{err.message});
    },
}
```

# [Borrowed and owned rows]($heading.id('rows'))

Rows borrow from the driver. Slices in a row stay valid until the result is
released or the stream advances:

```zig
var rows = try sqlz.unwrap(list_users.fetch(&conn, .{}));
defer rows.deinit();
while (try sqlz.unwrap(rows.next())) |user| {
    std.log.info("{d}: {s}", .{ user.id, user.name });
}
```

To keep a row, convert it explicitly with an allocator. A connection-level
owned-row scope can supply that allocator, for example an arena that frees
all rows at once.

# [Transactions and pools]($heading.id('transactions'))

Checked queries run against a connection, a transaction or a pool alike. See
the `basic_transaction`, `transaction_transfer` and `pooled_reads` examples.

# [Unchecked SQL]($heading.id('unchecked'))

SQL the checker never saw is still possible: `execute` and `fetch*` on the
adapter take a single statement, and `executeScript` runs a parameterless
script. The raw zqlite and pg.zig handles are available through `raw()` for
driver features sqlz doesn't model.
